Misc/CSRF-Testing-Checklist.content:
Pre-Flight Checks
If these aren’t met, move on.
Action is Privileged: Does it change state? (e.g., Change Email, Add Admin, Delete Account). Login/Search/Public forms don’t count.
Auth is Cookie-Based: Does the request use cookies for auth? (If it uses Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;, it is likely not vulnerable).
SameSite Policy: Are cookies set to SameSite=None or Lax? (If Strict, it’s likely secure).
No Unpredictable Headers: Are there custom headers required (e.g., X-Requested-With) that you cannot guess?

Testing Methodology
1. Basic Testing
Standard Attack: Create a basic HTML form submitting to the endpoint.
GET to POST: If the state change happens via GET (in URL), try it directly.
2. Content-Type Bypasses (JSON Endpoints)
If the endpoint expects JSON (application/json), the browser acts strictly. Try to fool it.
Change Content-Type: Change header to application/x-www-form-urlencoded. Does it still accept the data?
Text/Plain Method: Change header to text/plain and use a hidden input to craft the JSON string (see PoC section).
3. Method Bypasses
If the endpoint uses PUT, PATCH, or DELETE (which trigger CORS checks).
Method Swap: Change the method to POST. Does it still work?
Method Override: Use POST but add _method=PUT parameter or X-HTTP-Method-Override: PUT header.
4. Anti-CSRF Token Bypasses
If there is a csrf_token parameter.
Remove Token: Delete the entire parameter/header.
Blank Token: Send the parameter but leave the value empty.
Random Token: Change 1 character or send a random string of the same length.
Token Reuse: Use a token from a previous session (is it one-time use?).
Token Swapping: Use a valid token from Account B to attack Account A (is the token tied to the session?).
Decryption: Is the token just Base64 of the UserID or Timestamp? Decode it.
5. Referrer Header Bypasses
If the site checks the Referer header.
No Referrer: Add  to your PoC.
Directory Bypass: If they check if domain is present, try attacker.com/target.com.
Subdomain Bypass: If they allow subdomains, try target.attacker.com.

